Implementation note: the macOS translator

Ladybird translates with the public Translation framework, from Swift,
since the framework has no Objective-C API. Safari translates through
the same framework’s private Objective-C classes, e.g. _LTTranslator and
_LTTranslationSession. They reach translationd, whose online engine
(_LTOnlineTranslationEngine) translates on Apple’s servers when the
device can’t. But translationd accepts a client only if it has the
com.apple.private.translation entitlement, which Safari has and no other
developer can get, and rejects any other. So Ladybird translates on the
device only, into and from the languages the user has installed.
